      "knowledge_point_id": "b9017b4e-ddb3-56f6-ba30-b494a7e4e09f",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Orthogonal Matrices",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069920/assets/f6fb8d85c6e0921d49f16b410a3c3d7c549caabb166b0e65bb4c1982844310c3.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a square matrix\n\n- $A \\cdot A^T = I$\n\n- $A$ is an orthogonal matrix",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^T = A^T\\cdot A = I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069920/assets/3e419059afe53ef90b1c086f75c29513e7f6cc269ac4ecbf16e0db919222b3f5.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A\\cdot A^T = I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  },
